			<div class="content" id="post-content-4428757">
									

<p>This is very large and pretty dense but I have to commend you; nothing here feels like it's a waste of my time when I finished reading.</p>
<p>This piece does suffer from imprecisions in the clinical tone, however, there are no offenses that rise to the level of making me stop or causing me to have to reread.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4612-A is housed in an underground structure beneath Provisional Site-91, an 18th Century manor in Yorkshire, England, commonly referred to as Eckhart House.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sentences like this are messy. Part of this is also better suited for the ConProcs rather than buried down in the third paragraph of the description.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Most of the corpses were significantly charred and had warped skeletal structures due to exposure to significant heat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure 'charred' is a clinical word but I am not really going to fight on that. Also, bone doesn't melt so much as fall apart, so anything that's hot enough to &quot;warp&quot; it is going to just turn it into ash as well as any tissue attached to it.</p>
<p>Things like this dot the article; imprecise or inaccurate technical language that holds this piece back from being truly great.</p>
<p>Let me just clearly state, this is good. Although your final entry relies a lot on sensory description, I think your punch is great and this is a very solid +1 from me. This is good. Make no mistake. The amount of cohesion that goes into your whole big picture from the start to the end is at a level well above what I expected from an author posting (what looks to be) their first piece, and is deserving of its positive reception.</p>
<p>Really looking forward to how you grow from here. Good job.</p>

							</div>

								
					
