				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1775542&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200914" alt="augmentedspartan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1775542)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;" >augmentedspartan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1574873232 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Nov 2019 16:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4433041">
									

<p>I'm not too sure about this one. My biggest beef is that the themes don't seem to be fully explored, and are kinda just presented without really delving into them. Inhumane experimentation aside, I don't really see the negatives of scientific progress. We don't really have a real life equivalent for dissecting a god.</p>
<p>The other problem is that there isn't any discussion on what a god is. The interview presents the idea but doesn't go into any detail and is just left hanging. What <em>is</em> a god? Is it an omnipotent being? A powerful entity that has affected humanity in some way? Does it have to be fully supernatural? Is just being a powerful creature enough? The interview has both sides <em>mention</em> this concept but they don't go into any detail about either view. Dropping a question isn't the same as exploring a theme.</p>
<p>Then comes the reverence part. If we assume this type of entity is a god, what are the reasons why we should revere it? Just because they are powerful are they exempt of the inherent danger of animal behavior? It its morality universal or are they just as unimportant as any other living being? If it's divine, <em>why</em> shouldn't we use them to improve our species? All animals make their own priority number one, why should humans not do the same? We already dissect and experiment with human corpses, why should gods be any different? Is killing animals ok but not gods? What if another animal kills a god, are they in the wrong too?</p>
<p>I don't want to say the article is shallow, but the presentation of the themes feels superficial. I don't particularly care for stories that simply drop a theme and don't bother to expand on it. The piece should gave the reader something to think on, not just have them do all the work.</p>
<p>Not too keen on the Jesus allegory, either. Not because of having NotJesus himself, but because the image of a superhuman with glowing eyes takes away from what is otherwise a Lovecraftian entity. Not to mention that its entire role seems to be giving exposition at the end.</p>
<p>Reluctant downvote, but I feel like there's a good base here. The history table is where the article shone the most.</p>

							</div>

								
					
