														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4433165">
									

<p>First of all, thank you for reading and your input.</p>
<p>-B is not supposed to be Jesus, just FYI&#8230; Also what exposition does -B give?</p>
<p>Much of the exploration of the theme is supposed to be vague, as I didn’t want to get into exposition dumps anymore than I already had&#8230; my overall thoughts about it were less about reverence in regards to a god and more to a passed loved one, but I understand your point.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>-A is representative of a species unknown, which had the ability to manipulate energies (including thaumaturgy) you’ll notice in the footnote I talk about akiva radiation as a quantification of prime thaumaturgical force, stating it has been controversially called the evidence of divine grace. In my head gods are biological entities that are just capable of manipulating this prime thaumaturgical force to a much greater degree than people could even through study/runes/rituals.</p>
<p>-A was intimately involved in the creation of the solar system, tasked with making sure various systems such as ecology and weather systems worked properly. It stuck around while it’s Siblings took off, and got very interested in developing organisms like people&#8230; but like all parents looking down on the next generation, -A had a different sort of idea of how they should behave. It saw the increasing technological and anomalous abilities as a threat to the survival of the species, so it stepped in from time to time.</p>
<p>-A made -B by manipulating the genetic code of an anatomically modern (but very ancient) human baby&#8230; so it could have eyes on the ground, and agent to be involved in the perspective of humans.</p>
<p>Dr. Varga is uninterested in the discussion of deities. She thinks this is an interesting subject to be treated as any new species would be. This one is just more anomalous than a new species of crab or wolf. You can see this in her interaction with the POI and in that footnote I mentioned above, also in that she wishes to test the genetic material in more experimental ways.</p>
<p>The serpents hand and -B think this is something more to be revered, both for their own reasons. But the idea that -B lashed out at the occultists was one of mourning. How badly someone freaks out when they lose a loved one.</p>
<p>-B doesn’t kill Douglas because he doesn’t see anything wrong with investigating the body of his father. -B thinks progress is inevitable, and does not share the perspective of his papa. In that way he’s fairly rebellious, as he has not been running around for the last 200 years fucking with human progress (given the incredible leaps we have made since the industrial revolution)</p>
<p>Also it’s been enough time, he’s moved on. What he doesn’t approve of is the using of his father’s body as some sort of ritual, as that would be a desecration. Not of a god but of his family.</p>
<p>Thus, the dichotomy is this: push forward progress at whatever the cost (as is almost always the case) vs. treating the dead with respect. The fact that the dead in this story is, or was, a god is of secondary concern.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hope this clarified or at least was interesting.<br />
Thanks for reading!</p>

