<p>But I absolutely despise when everything is spelled out for me in a story, especially a short story… horror for me is only catching glimpses, and I think that can work for a contemplative piece as well.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh, yes, I agree, but to me this piece felt less like horror and more like contemplative fiction. When using a fictional story to explore complex themes, I prefer it when the story gives a lot of different views but also explains how one could come up with such view. So the questioning comes less about the concept itself, but the different interpretations of said concept.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So honestly, it still feels like a success even with your downvote.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's good. A single negative opinion shouldn't stop you from appreciating your own work. Plus, even if I don't like this particular piece, contrasting views can always make for interesting new art, so the next article could be the one that wins me over.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thanks for taking the time to comment, also. No sarcasm, it was legitimately interesting to examine my work through your critiques.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No problem. I wanted to give my opinion because I enjoyed certain aspects of the article, so for me it wasn't &quot;a bad&quot; article, just not my thing overall.</p>

